{
  "name": "PM API Literacy Lab — City to Umbrella Recommendation",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "# Product Leader API / Workflow Literacy Lab\n\n**Scenario:** Should I carry an umbrella tomorrow?\n\nThis workflow uses the same Open-Meteo geocoding + forecast contract as the companion Postman exercise.\n\n### What to notice\n1. A human input (`Stamford`) is not what the weather API needs.\n2. One API translates the city into latitude/longitude.\n3. Those values become inputs to a second API.\n4. The workflow selects **tomorrow** from daily arrays.\n5. A product rule turns data into a recommendation.\n6. Error paths are part of the product behavior, not an afterthought.\n\n### Output boundary\nFor the **manual learning path**, the result simply appears in n8n node output.  \nThe optional **webhook path** returns the same JSON to Postman only to demonstrate that n8n can expose a callable interface.\n\nThis lab intentionally does **not** build a webpage, dashboard, mobile notification, device action, or AI handoff. In real products, one of those downstream destinations is often the whole point of the workflow.\n\n**No paid external API or subscription credential is required by this workflow.** Open-Meteo's public endpoints are used without an API key for this learning exercise.",
        "height": 552,
        "width": 1050,
        "color": 5
      },
      "id": "c64d2cac-ecf9-44d4-8783-4c4403e68dcd",
      "name": "README — What This Workflow Teaches",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        0,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## 1. Two ways to start the same workflow\n\n**Manual Trigger** is best while learning. It supplies `Stamford` so you can click **Execute workflow** and inspect every node.\n\n**Webhook Trigger** is the optional callable version. From Postman, send:\n\n`GET <test-or-production-webhook-url>?city=Stamford`\n\nClick on the Webhook Trigger to find the URL, then go to Postman to enter it. Execute the workflow and then call it from Postman with HTTP. Remember to add a city parameter to the end of the URL.\n\nBoth entry paths converge on the same product logic. That is deliberate: one canonical workflow, not three drifting copies.",
        "height": 346,
        "width": 680,
        "color": 3
      },
      "id": "78e1a21e-df41-4074-a3cd-988a673e5d12",
      "name": "1 — Two Ways to Start",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        0,
        624
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## 2. Human place name → machine coordinates\n\nThe user thinks in **city names**. The forecast API needs **latitude + longitude**.\n\nThe first HTTP Request calls Open-Meteo geocoding:\n\n- `name = city`\n- `count = 1`\n- `language = en`\n- `format = json`\n\n**PM exercise:** change the city to `Springfield` and temporarily change `count` to `5`. Multiple valid matches immediately create a product decision about disambiguation.",
        "height": 330,
        "width": 920,
        "color": 7
      },
      "id": "5027ba79-c97a-43b8-aa8d-95d6752d782e",
      "name": "2 — Geocoding Is an Interface Translation",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        1152,
        480
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## 3. One interface contract feeds another\n\nThe geocoding response contains latitude and longitude.  \nThe workflow extracts those values and maps them into the forecast request.\n\nThe forecast call intentionally matches the Postman article:\n\n**Current**\n- `temperature_2m`\n- `apparent_temperature`\n- `precipitation`\n\n**Daily**\n- `temperature_2m_max`\n- `temperature_2m_min`\n- `precipitation_probability_max`\n\n`timezone=auto`\n\nThis is the central architectural lesson: **the output contract of one interface becomes the input contract of another.**",
        "height": 360,
        "width": 980,
        "color": 7
      },
      "id": "39c7d155-a52e-47ff-bf35-16b0d4a7a7c4",
      "name": "3 — One API Output Becomes Another API Input",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        2272,
        480
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## 4. Data becomes a product decision\n\nOpen-Meteo returns daily values as parallel arrays. Index `0` is today; this lab selects index `1` for **tomorrow**.\n\nThen an IF node applies the intentionally simple rule:\n\n**If tomorrow's maximum precipitation probability > 60%, recommend bringing an umbrella.**\n\nThe IF node is implementation.  \n**The 60% threshold is a product decision.**\n\nTry changing it to 40% or 80% and observe how product behavior changes without changing either external API.",
        "height": 360,
        "width": 950,
        "color": 7
      },
      "id": "86e5b6d7-5980-4794-958b-8ea12fc6310d",
      "name": "4 — Product Logic Lives Between Systems",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        3376,
        480
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## 5. Break the happy path\n\nThis workflow handles several failure modes:\n\n- blank city input\n- city returns no usable location\n- geocoding HTTP request fails\n- forecast HTTP request fails\n- forecast response lacks tomorrow's required data\n\nNotice that technical failures are converted into a small, readable JSON result with an `error_code`, `message`, and suggested HTTP status.\n\nIn production you would also decide:\n- what the user sees\n- what gets logged\n- whether/when to retry\n- whether to offer alternate location matches\n- whether to fall back to another provider",
        "height": 428,
        "width": 1180,
        "color": 4
      },
      "id": "dcfbf152-3c19-44f4-9ed5-c9631f350381",
      "name": "5 — Failure Paths Are Product Paths",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        2048,
        1600
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## 6. This lab stops at the workflow boundary\n\n**Manual run:** click **Manual Result - Inspect Output** and read the JSON.\n\n**Webhook run:** the same JSON is returned to Postman.\n\nWe are **not** building a user-facing display or sending the result onward.\n\nA real product might next:\n- render it in a web/mobile UI\n- send a notification\n- trigger another workflow or device\n- store it\n- pass context to an AI/model\n- combine it with other data\n\nThose are common purposes of orchestration, but they are intentionally out of scope so the interface mechanics remain visible.",
        "height": 390,
        "width": 920,
        "color": 5
      },
      "id": "12c7cfff-104b-41e6-8c28-efbccf34189e",
      "name": "6 — Where the Result Goes",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        4352,
        592
      ]
    },
